WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:05.193
I thought I'd be there forever
until the day I did it.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.033
You know, it's funny.

00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:08.710
I never had a conversation with my dad

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:10.110
about me leaving day-to-day.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.080
It just kind of happened.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.233
It was like crazy to think about.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.620
Was also a time where,

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:19.980
probably the toughest time my career

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.640
was with my dad and I weren't clicking.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.700
There was a point where
the business got so big

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.290
and I was getting the credit

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:30.150
and I was starting to get
the credit in the press

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.440
and I feel like I could
have done a better job

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.950
of giving my dad more credit

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:37.200
but I think at some level

00:00:38.509 --> 00:00:40.980
(sighs) I was struggling subconsciously

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.710
without even realizing it
that I owned nothing of it.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.970
And now I'm married

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:50.320
and you know, here I am
at 30, 31, 32 years old,

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.240
I'm not paying myself
a lot of money at all.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.280
You kinda wake up and you've put,

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.000
you know, a decade of 15-hour days,

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.290
360 days a year into something,

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:03.920
you've built something and watched it grow

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.670
from 4 to 60 million in revenue.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:07.670
And you've never you've never really made

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.020
more than $150 000 a year.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.120
I just started getting
so much of the credit,

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.210
that it started undermining

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.680
my dad's own feelings for himself.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.400
It's almost like he was
getting wiped out of history

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.393
and it was an interesting difficult time.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.450
What it took for me to
get outta that moment was

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.920
the ability to start over.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.790
(dramatic music)

00:01:32.421 --> 00:01:35.420
- [Director] Did you feel
like there was a point where

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:38.040
you felt maybe unfulfilled by what,

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.540
even though you were fulfilled,

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.189
helping your dad doing all this,

00:01:42.189 --> 00:01:43.134
you were unfulfilled.

00:01:43.134 --> 00:01:45.110
There was something else
that was there for you,

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:46.660
that you wanted to chase,

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:48.726
you wanted to pursue it?

00:01:48.726 --> 00:01:52.290
- Yeah, I mean I could
feel like, me changing.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:53.750
You know, the obsession with building

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:58.000
the biggest wine retail
company in America was fading.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.420
And my curiosity about this Web 2.0

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.690
evolution of the Internet was rising.

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:06.020
And I was starting to realize

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.470
it wasn't that I was
a great wine merchant,

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.460
it was that I was a great communicator.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.299
And, my career started to shift.

00:02:14.299 --> 00:02:17.049
(dramatic music)

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.610
At some levels, you don't realize

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.590
when something is dying
down when you're in it.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.470
And I didn't realize how much

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:31.580
fire something new and fresh

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.300
and wide open and so grand, you know,

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:38.060
I'm coming from the wine
business, which is incredible

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.290
and I missed it so much
I got back into it,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.450
but this whole world of like,

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.870
this is the changing media landscape,

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.670
this is the communication change,

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.470
this is going to affect the world.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:52.260
I often use the analogy of a lightning bug

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.170
that just goes to the
light and gets zapped.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.003
I always wondered,

00:02:56.003 --> 00:02:58.090
does that bug know that
they're about to get zapped

00:02:58.090 --> 00:02:59.630
but they just can't help it?

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:03.440
There's an enormous sense of destiny

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.763
that runs through my body.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.160
You know, I just

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.233
I just feel it.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.300
The most important thing I'd think about

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.730
when I think about getting to happiness

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.200
is not so much owning anything.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.603
It's the ability to not
hear anything but yourself.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.840
There's nothing more powerful than

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.920
when everything gets quiet

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.720
and the only thing that penetrates

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:36.063
is your own voice and that
voice isn't judging you.

00:03:37.826 --> 00:03:41.197
- [Director] When you look back now,

00:03:41.197 --> 00:03:44.070
at the kid who felt pressure

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.123
or the kid who felt an obligation.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.503
what would you say that kid now?

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:52.570
- I would tell that kid

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:55.473
to stay the course 'cause
he's gotta figure it out.

